From 84ee380aa93f26f830e9d556d6c7aead790d0c2b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 11:41:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Split the guarded tree's root box by what consumes it

0103 counted the root at 61.79% of the corpus's conversions and left the
phase one question. The root is boxed only because its consumer takes a
JSValue, so the only thing that removes it is a consumer able to take
the raw double the tree already holds -- and which consumers those are
is knowable only to the compiler, since the box is minted at the tree
and what receives it is not visible from there.

The consumer travels with the node being visited, and only to a node
that IS an AstBinaryExpression -- the one shape that reaches the tree
builder directly. That restriction is the safety argument: the field
cannot survive into a nested visit because it is never set for a node
whose compilation performs one. The tree builder clears it for its own
construction for the same reason, since a leaf may contain a tree. A
compound assignment does not claim its right-hand side either, because
there the tree is an operand of the compound operator rather than the
value stored.

Counted over all fifteen suites, of 42 849 742 root boxes:

  local      19 006 647   44.36%
  element     7 673 079   17.91%
  property    5 631 192   13.14%
  argument    1 897 892    4.43%
  return        169 872    0.40%
  unattributed 8 471 060  19.77%

The dominant consumer is a LOCAL, not an element and not a property. So
the remaining prize is not a typed backing store and not an unboxed
shape slot: a proven-numeric local already has a raw double home from
item 3-3, and a root landing in a local is one the existing numeric tier
failed to type. The typed store's whole ceiling on root boxes is the
element row -- 17.91% of them, 11.07% of the corpus's conversions --
against 0113's finding that it is an allocation wash at the corpus
read/write ratio.

Per suite the split is not uniform and that is the useful part. Crypto
sends 81.8% of its roots to a local, NavierStokes 59.2% to an element,
Typescript 74.0% to a property, Splay 98.5% to a call argument.

The 19.77% residual is reported rather than folded away: it is the
assignment forms this pass does not wire -- destructuring, shadowed
bindings, and expression positions with no store at all -- and Gameboy
carries 43% of its roots there, so its row is the least trustworthy in
the table.

7 further cases, the load-bearing one being that a consumer does not
leak into a tree nested inside its own right-hand side. 8 162 tests
across eight projects, no failures.
